<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jurchens</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jurchen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jurchens were a Tungusic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. In the twelfth century they founded the Jin dynasty, which conquered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>. Their descendants, unified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> and renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jurchens.jpg" alt="Jurchens" width="268" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jurchen_woodblock_print.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Jurchens were a number of Tungusic-speaking peoples who lived in the part of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. They first entered Chinese records in 748, in the forests and river valleys of what is now divided between Heilongjiang Province and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Primorsky Krai. Scholarship since the Qing period has traced their origin to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> tribes, among them the Heishui or Blackwater Mohe, but this remains conjectural. They were culturally diverse, with groups living as hunter-gatherers, semi-nomadic pastoralists or settled farmers, and they generally lacked a central authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 11th century the Jurchens were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Khitan rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. Those near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> had been tributaries of Goryeo since the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Taejo of Goryeo</a>, and they switched allegiance between Liao and Goryeo as it suited them, offering tribute to both courts for the material rewards it brought.</p><p>In 1115 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Wanyan Aguda</a>, chief of the Wanyan tribe, united the Jurchen tribes and declared himself emperor. The dynasty took the name Jin, meaning gold, after the Gold River of the Jurchen homeland. In 1120 he seized Shangjing, the northern capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>. In the Jin–Song Wars the Jurchens overran most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, captured the Northern Song capital Bianjing in 1127 and settled on a border with the Southern Song along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>. Jurchens who settled in towns intermarried with other peoples, and the Jin rulers came to follow Confucian norms.</p><p>Under the Ming, Chinese officials sorted the Jurchens into three groups: the Jianzhou, the Haixi and the so-called Wild Jurchens. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> found allies among them against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and set up 178 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. From 1586 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>, a chieftain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jianzhou-jurchens">Jianzhou Jurchens</a>, united the tribes over some 30 years. In 1635 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> renamed his people the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, a break from their past as Chinese vassals, and the dynasty founded the next year became the Qing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, the Jurchens prized horsemanship, archery and hunting, and they used the title khan and the term beile for a powerful chief. Unlike the Mongols, most were sedentary farmers of grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> who also kept oxen, pigs, sheep and horses. They did not observe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, so any capable son or nephew could become leader. They practised shamanic rituals and revered a sky goddess, and under the Jin many turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Wanyan Xiyin devised a Jurchen script in 1120, based on the Khitan script, but it died out soon after the Jin fell. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>'s followers were organised into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a> and adopted a script based on Mongolian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jurchen people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Khitan Liao state absorbed most of its territory and employed former Balhae officials, refugees strengthened the new Korean kingdom of Goryeo, and the <b>Jurchens</b>, who later founded the Jin dynasty, descended from Mohe peoples of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign trade passed through the port of Byeongnando near Kaegyong, visited by Song Chinese, Japanese, Khitan and <b>Jurchen</b> traders and, in the eleventh century, by Arab merchants, through whom the name Goryeo probably travelled west to become Korea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jin dynasty was founded by the <b>Jurchen</b>, a Tungusic people of the forests and river valleys of Manchuria who lived by farming, hunting, fishing and stock-raising and paid tribute to the Khitan Liao dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…in Sejong's reign, the retired king Taejong sent an expedition against Tsushima to suppress Japanese pirates, and campaigns against the <b>Jurchen</b> in the 1430s and 1440s established the four commanderies and six garrisons that fixed the northern border on the Yalu and Tumen rivers, where it still lies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…such as the Xi and the tribes of Mongolia, herded horses, sheep and cattle and hunted, and the <b>Jurchen</b> of Manchuria fished, farmed and traded furs, while in the south the Sixteen Prefectures and the Liao valley held a large population of Chinese farmers, along with Balhae settlers…</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nurhaci's Banner system organised the entire <b>Jurchen</b> population into colour-coded units that were at once army formations, census registers and social structures.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
